---
// The sample lesson itself: both parts, and the line that ties it to our
// policy work. Shown in full on the Teaching page and on its own printable
// page (teaching/sample-lesson.astro), so the words live here once. The
// underscore keeps this file from becoming a page of its own.
//
// Deliberately text-only: the game is named, but no character art, logos or
// screenshots appear. The figures are read off the children's own screens
// rather than published here, so the lesson never goes stale on a balance
// patch and we never present numbers as though they were official game data.
interface Props {
  /** The heading level of each part: 3 inside the Teaching page, 2 on the lesson's own page. */
  level?: 2 | 3;
}
const Heading = Astro.props.level === 2 ? 'h2' : 'h3';
---

<div class="lesson">
  <article class="rail lesson-part">
    <div class="rail-head">
      <p class="part-tag">Part 1 · Maths · 30 min</p>
      <Heading>Bigger isn't better</Heading>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="objective">
        <b>Objective</b> Students work out a rate and explain why a bigger single number can still lose
        to a smaller one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each student picks two characters they actually play in <cite>Brawl Stars</cite> and reads the
        damage per shot and reload speed from their own screen. They almost always pick one slow, heavy
        hitter and one fast, weaker one, and they almost always assume the bigger number wins.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then they work out the damage per second, and the faster character usually comes out ahead. That
        surprise is the lesson: one number on its own doesn't tell you much, and the number that matters
        is usually a rate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the twist: the faster character can only hit enemies standing close by. Is it still the
        better choice? Now the answer depends on how you play, and that's what Part 2 is for.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        <b>Extension</b> How much would the reload speed have to change for the answer to flip? It's
        their first go at solving for an unknown, and they get there because they're curious, not because
        a textbook told them to.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        <b>Watch for</b> Students who get the sums right but can't explain why the answer surprised them.
        The explanation is the goal; the multiplication is just how we get there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="rail lesson-part">
    <div class="rail-head">
      <p class="part-tag">Part 2 · English · 30 min</p>
      <Heading>Make the case</Heading>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="objective">
        <b>Objective</b> Students write a short argument with a claim, evidence, a fair version of the
        other side, and a recommendation.
      </p>
      <p>
        They argue which of their two characters is better, using the numbers they just worked out as
        evidence. There's one firm rule: they have to write one honest sentence about why someone might
        reasonably pick the other character. If they can't, they don't fully understand their own
        argument yet.
      </p>
      <p>Sentence starters go on the board for children learning English as a second language:</p>
      <ul class="frames">
        <li>I think ______ is better because ______.</li>
        <li>The evidence is ______.</li>
        <li>Someone might choose ______ instead, because ______.</li>
        <li>Even so, I would recommend ______ because ______.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Then they follow the Think First steps. Each student asks an AI tool to check their calculation
        and give one hint to strengthen their argument, and decides for themselves whether the feedback
        is right. Finally, with the AI closed, they explain their recommendation to the group in their
        own words.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four or five sentences is a finished piece. Two or three students read theirs out, and everyone
        else's job is to spot the counterargument, not to decide who won.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        <b>Watch for</b> A counterargument that's really a put-down, like "someone might choose it
        because they're bad at the game." Send it back. Learning to describe the other side fairly is the
        whole point.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- The line that makes the two halves of AFG one organisation -->
  <div class="rail lesson-part">
    <div class="rail-head"></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Claim, evidence, an honest case against, then a recommendation: it's the same structure we use
        in <a href="/policy#how-we-write">every proposal we send to the government</a>. We teach
        eleven-year-olds the same method we use with policymakers.
      </p>
      <p>
        It goes the other way too. Our December 2025 proposal,{' '}
        <a href="/policy/think-first">Think First</a>, takes the method our tutors use every week and
        proposes it for every primary school in Hong Kong.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
